'''''Chiu Bûn-hái''''' (周文海; 1 April 1474 – 9 June 1543) was a Zong merchant of [[Huwei]] and the founder of the merchant house of the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]]. He took the grant of land at the bay of [[Oktalwa]] in 1511, arranged the marriage of his son [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]] to [[Chinowa]], daughter of the Akwesa ruler [[Hokta]], in 1521, and thereby established the alliance from which the polity of [[Okheng]] was formed.

He was born in the year in which [[Anping]] was founded and died in the year in which the colonial territory was divided, outliving by sixteen years the chiefdom whose land he had acquired, and surviving to see his son convey Okheng to the [[Zong colonial administration]] for 4,000 taels of gold.

== Early life ==

Chiu Bûn-hái was born at [[Huwei]], the capital of [[Zong]], on 1 April 1474. Nothing is recorded of his parentage or his early life.

He was a merchant of that city by the end of the 15th century. The date on which he established the trading house that became the Sūn-heng Hâng is not recorded, and the house first appears in the colonial papers in connection with the grant of 1511, by which time it was an established concern with the shipping and capital to sustain a settlement at the far end of the [[Shendan Ocean]].

== Grant of 1511 ==

In 1511 the Akwesa ruler Hokta granted a plot of land at the bay of Oktalwa to a party of Zong traders in exchange for ten pigs. The grantees were Chiu Bûn-hái and the Sūn-heng Hâng, and the transaction was recorded by the Zong as a purchase. Akwesa practice did not treat land as property that could be alienated, and what was granted was the use of the site.

A depot was built on the plot and was operated as a manned outpost from that year. Chiu Bûn-hái was then 37. He appears to have removed his family to the settlement rather than directing the venture from Zong: his son, born in 1505, was brought up at the bay from the age of six and appears in the settlement's records from that period.

== Alliance ==

The company's position at the bay depended on the goodwill of the chiefdom that held the surrounding country, and from 1511 the Sūn-heng Hâng assisted the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms. Hokta's territory expanded in consequence, and the arrangement was of advantage to both parties: the company supplied men, shipping and money; the chiefdom supplied land, legitimacy and access.

In 1521 the alliance was sealed by a marriage. Chiu Bûn-hái's son [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], then sixteen, was married to Hokta's daughter [[Chinowa]], then about fourteen. The marriage was arranged between the two older men; the colonial papers describe it as an agreement between them rather than as a matter between the couple. With the Akwesa succession following the male line and Hokta having no sons, the match also supplied the chiefdom with a male figure of standing connected to Hokta's line.

By the 1520s the outpost had become the centre of a territory acquired through the company's support of the Akwesa, and it was governed jointly by the merchant family and the chiefly family.

== Objection of the colonial administration ==

Word of the arrangement reached [[Anping]] about 1522. The administration objected to the political character of what Chiu Bûn-hái had built rather than to the marriage, which was a matter of private conduct. It held that he was conducting diplomacy, waging war and expanding a territory in his own name, and dealing with a foreign people other than on behalf of Zong.

The administration took no action. Its stated grounds were that the acknowledgment of the arrangement would concede the acquisition of a polity by a private merchant, and that its suppression would require an expedition to the far end of the coast. The correspondence was retained at Anping.

Chiu Bûn-hái appears less often in the colonial papers after this date. No document written by him is known, and the record of the territory's government in the 1520s and 1530s is largely that of the administration's correspondence about it.

== Okheng ==

Hokta died in 1527 of plague, in an outbreak that killed many of the Akwesa and left the chiefdom much reduced. Chiu Bûn-hái was 53. His son succeeded to the chieftainship and became the ruler of the merged territory, which was known to the Zong as [[Okheng]] (沃興) — a name formed from the first element of Oktalwa and the second of Sūn-heng, and so carrying the company's name within that of the polity it had made.

Chiu Bûn-hái's own position after 1527 is not recorded. The company continued to supply the men and shipping on which the territory depended and he remained the head of the merchant house, but the rule of Okheng was his son's and the colonial papers treat the younger man as its ruler.

== Sale of 1535 ==

In 1535 the colonial administration determined to acquire the site as the harbour for its northern presence. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k, by then the ruler of Okheng, was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was at the same time threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should he refuse. No charge was laid.

Chiu Bûn-hái was 61 and was alive when the territory was conveyed. The land sold was the plot he had taken in 1511 for ten pigs, and the territory was the one he had spent twenty-four years enlarging through the company's support of the Akwesa. The sum paid was assessed as the value of the works the company had built, and no allowance was made for the land, the political entity or the trade.

The transaction was recorded as a purchase.

== Death ==

He died at [[Tamsui]] on 9 June 1543, aged 69. He had survived Hokta by sixteen years.

His death fell in the year of the [[Tamsui Plot]], in which his son took part and was removed from the office of provisional administrator, and of the division of the colonial territory along the [[Qingjiang]] river into the [[Sinchew Colony]] and the [[Louyang Colony]]. He did not live to see the end of that year.

== Family ==

He was the father of [[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], his only recorded child, born in 1505. Through his son's marriage to [[Chinowa]] he was connected to the chiefly family of the Akwesa, and his two grandchildren — unnamed in the colonial papers — were the grandchildren of Hokta.

His wife is not recorded, and neither is the date of his own marriage. His daughter-in-law [[Chinowa]] outlived him by forty-six years and his son by fifty-five.

== Historical record ==

No document written by Chiu Bûn-hái or at his direction is known. He appears in the colonial papers only where the administration had occasion to write about him: the grant of 1511, the arrangement of 1522 that the administration judged not worth managing, and the transactions by which the land he had acquired passed out of his family's hands.

The evidence for his purposes is therefore the evidence of what he did, and the papers of the administration that declined to intervene in it. The merchant house he founded, the alliance he arranged, and the polity that carried his company's name are recorded; his own account of any of them is not.

